{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"The rebased main tier caught two more of mine. Both fixed; both worth recording because one is\na repeat of a mistake I had already made and named in this same ticket.\n\n1. `src/rebar/_config_schema.py` hit 808 LOC, over the 800 hard cap — my CompactConfig comment\n   block pushed it past. The cap is absolute and deliberately has no allowlist. Fixed by\n   compressing the schema comments to four lines that point at where the rationale already\n   lives (`compact_trigger`'s module docstring and `docs/config.md`), which is the better home\n   for it anyway — the schema should say WHAT the key is, not argue the design. 799 now.\n\n2. `test_the_close_holds_no_store_lock_across_the_trigger` failed, and the TEST was wrong, not\n   the code. I wrote it with `trigger=always` and the comment \"worst case: fold inline, not\n   detached\". That reasoning is backwards: `always` exists precisely to run the fold INLINE so\n   the suite can observe it, so of course the folding process holds the store lock while it\n   folds. I asserted a production property against a test-only mode, and it proved nothing —\n   it only started failing once the trigger actually began firing in that test.\n\n   Rewritten to exercise `async`, the production mode, with the spawn stubbed: the close must\n   write NO fold artifact and never be seen holding the lock while one exists, and it must have\n   DELEGATED (the spawn recorded). That is the property that matters.\n\n   This is the second test in this ticket that overclaimed — the first was the convergence test\n   that never merged, caught by the code reviewer. Same root error both times: I wrote the\n   assertion I wanted to be true rather than the one the setup could actually establish. Worth\n   naming rather than quietly fixing.\n\nAlso: a 101-char line tripped E501 and the pre-commit hook correctly refused the amend, which\nis the gate doing its job — I nearly recorded a \"committed\" state that had not committed.\n\nGates green after both fixes: ruff clean, mypy 526 files, complexity ratchet unchanged,\nmodule-size contract passing. Re-running the full main tier on the fixed stack before pushing."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786619200649943001,"uuid":"913eecd4-acff-4b5a-87e5-1e0fe17b0959"}